  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">BABICORA LAGOON IV</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Babcora Lagoon, one of the most threatened wetlands in the Mexican Central Highlands, shelters the highest concentration of cranes and waterfowl travelling the Central Flyway. This wetland is important for Sandhill Cranes, Long-billed Curlew and Mountain Plover. It hosts very high concentrations of Snow Goose and Ross&apos;s Goose, as well as high numbers of White-fronted Goose, Northern Pintail, Blue-winged Teal and Green-winged Teal. The project actions will improve the quality of water draining into the wetland, reduce the amount of sediment and increase available habitat for migratory birds. In previous phases, partners had reduced contamination and sediments at several sites in the basin; this phase builds on that. This project will set up an ecological easement on 2,471 acres. It will also clear 3.7 miles of natural channels, build or rehabilitate 12 miles of fencing to improve grassland management, develop livestock management plans for communal land owners, monitor birds, and carry out environmental education.</narrative>
  </description>
